      "anchor": "q_t(x,t) + A q_x(x,t) = 0; q : R × R → R^m",
      "observation": "The governing system is homogeneous, first order, linear, and has constant coefficients in one spatial dimension. Its unknown is an m-component real vector, and A is a constant real m × m matrix. Subscripts denote partial derivatives."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 23; printed page 1; equations (1.2)–(1.3) and following paragraph",
      "anchor": "q(x,t) = q̃(x − ū t)",
      "observation": "For scalar advection, the source identifies a translated profile as a solution and describes propagation with constant speed ū and unchanged shape."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 24; printed page 2; equations (1.5)–(1.6)",
      "anchor": "p_t + K u_x = 0; u_t + (1/ρ)p_x = 0",
      "observation": "The acoustic example has q = (p,u), with p the pressure perturbation, u the particle velocity, K the bulk modulus, and ρ the density. Its constant coefficient matrix has rows (0,K) and (1/ρ,0)."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 24; printed page 2; characteristic combinations after equation (1.6)",
      "anchor": "w¹ = p + ρcu; w² = p − ρcu; c = √(K/ρ)",
      "observation": "The source states that these combinations satisfy w¹_t + c w¹_x = 0 and w²_t − c w²_x = 0, respectively. It interprets c as the speed of sound and −c as leftward propagation."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25; printed page 3; first paragraph",
      "anchor": "hyperbolic if the matrix A has real eigenvalues and a corresponding set of m linearly independent eigenvectors",
      "observation": "The source defines hyperbolicity for the constant coefficient system by the existence of real eigenvalues and a complete set of linearly independent eigenvectors."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25; printed page 3; first paragraph",
      "anchor": "any vector in R^m can be uniquely decomposed as a linear combination of these eigenvectors",
      "observation": "The source states unique vector decomposition in the chosen eigenvector basis and says this provides the decomposition into distinct waves, referring forward to Chapter 3."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25; printed page 3; first paragraph; selected sentence",
      "anchor": "The corresponding eigenvalues of A give the wave speeds at which each wave propagates.",
      "observation": "Each component wave in the eigenvector decomposition propagates with the corresponding eigenvalue as its speed."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25; printed page 3; end of first paragraph",
      "anchor": "the acoustics matrix A of (1.6) has eigenvalues −c and +c",
      "observation": "The source illustrates the general claim by identifying the two acoustic eigenvalues with the propagation speeds of the acoustic waves."
    }
  ],
